{"step_index":212,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:37:46Z","thinking":"**Verifying Key Mathematical Properties**\n\nThe elaboration successfully verified several key lemmas related to ball-within properties and their relationship to concepts like eccentricity and cardinality. Specifically, lemmas concerning `minUpTo_le`, `radius_le_eccentricity`, `ballWithin_subset_*`, and the implications of ball-within equality with `succ` have been confirmed. This also covers lemmas related to the cardinality of `ballWithin` with `succ` and other conditions. The result also confirmed the `eccentricity_` result.\n\n\n**Examining Relationship of Graph Properties**\n\nFocusing on the inequality `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f`, I must determine the implications of `romanWeight f` being less than the cardinality of the vertex set. Further, evaluating if the graph radius is less than or equal to one is crucial to derive useful insights.\n\nIf the radius is at most one, then the radius plus one is at most two. The goal is to prove that roman weight is always greater or equal to two for nontrivial classes. If the Roman weight is less than or equal to one, then there must be no vertices assigned the value of two, which leads to a contradiction due to the Roman domination property.\n\nThus, if no vertex has a value of two, then no vertex can have a value of zero, implying all vertices have values of at least one due to the range of the function, since all vertices must either have 0 or 2 as the value and we know that 2 does not exist, so 0 is not allowed. This leads to the conclusion that the Roman weight must be greater or equal to two.\n\n\n**Establishing Lower Bound on Roman Weight**\n\nThe initial constraint on the Roman weight yielded a contradiction when assuming a weight of 1, establishing a lower bound of 2.  Furthermore, when the radius is at most 1, it directly follows that the radius plus one is less than or equal to the Roman weight.\n\nA further exploration seeks the minimum Roman weight when the radius is greater or equal to 2, considering the possibility of a Roman weight of 2. Considering this case, it leads to contradictions either when all vertices have a positive value or only one has a value.\n\nTherefore, if the radius is greater or equal to two, then the roman weight is greater or equal to 3. If the radius is exactly 2, then radius plus one is 3, which is also less or equal to roman weight.\n\n\n**Generalizing Graph Radius and Roman Weight**\n\nIf the graph radius is at least 3, the Roman weight is at least 4. This is proven by contradiction, considering scenarios where the Roman weight is 3 and deriving contradictions with radius constraints. Specifically, two cases arise; both lead to the radius being less or equal to 2, which contradicts the initial assumption.\n\nA generalization is considered: if Roman weight is less or equal to *k*, then the radius is less or equal to *k-1*. This implies `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f`, which is what's being proven. The proof strategy will evaluate the `two_le_romanWeight` property.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"cat << 'EOF' > /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_weight.lean\\nimport Mathlib\\nimport LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\\n\\nopen SimpleGraph\\nopen GraphCalc\\n\\nlemma two_le_romanWeight {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\\n    (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) (f : V → Fin 3)\\n    (hf : GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) :\\n    2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanWeight f := by\\n  unfold GraphCalc.romanWeight\\n  unfold GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass at hnt\\n  unfold GraphCalc.order at hnt\\n  by_contra hc\\n  have h_le1 : ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≤ 1 := by omega\\n  have h_no2 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 2 := by\\n    intro v hv\\n    have h_mem := Finset.single_le_sum (fun u _ => Nat.zero_le (f u : ℕ)) (Finset.mem_univ v)\\n    rw [hv] at h_mem\\n    omega\\n  have h_no0 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 0 := by\\n    intro v hv\\n    have h_f0 : f v = 0 := by\\n      have hval := (f v).isLt\\n      ext\\n      exact hv\\n    obtain ⟨u, _, hu2⟩ := hf v h_f0\\n    have hu2_nat : (f u : ℕ) = 2 := by\\n      rw [hu2]\\n      rfl\\n    exact h_no2 u hu2_nat\\n  have h_ge1 : ∀ v : V, 1 ≤ (f v : ℕ) := by\\n    intro v\\n    have h0 := h_no0 v\\n    omega\\n  have h_sum_ge : ∑ v : V, 1 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) :=\\n    Finset.sum_le_sum (fun v _ => h_ge1 v)\\n  rw [Finset.sum_const, nsmul_eq_mul, mul_one] at h_sum_ge\\n  rw [Finset.card_univ] at h_sum_ge\\n  omega\\nEOF\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Running command\"","toolSummary":"\"Write test_weight.lean\""}}]}
